---
title: Data Transforms
description: Typed, eager transforms for reusable application data.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms are ordinary functions: source rows in, typed derived data out.\nThey do not rewrite mark options, retain state, cache results, or own framework\nreactivity."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"functions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Functions"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Export"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fold"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wide rows repeated into authored field/value pairs"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Named group fields, reducer outputs, and lineage"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric intervals on one axis"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binXY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric cells with x and y intervals"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binTimeX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binTimeY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calendar-aligned intervals from a supplied time interval"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollingWindow"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat input rows extended with rolling outputs"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cumulative"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat input rows extended with running outputs"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rank"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat input rows extended with ranks"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat input rows extended with normalized values"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Selected original rows"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stackRowsX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"stackRowsY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Flat input rows extended with stack endpoints"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mosaicX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mosaicY"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two normalized proportional interval dimensions"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxRows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tukey summary and outlier rows by category"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linearRegressionRowsX/Y"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sampled least-squares fits and confidence bounds"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waterfall"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordered signed contributions as cumulative intervals"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"quantile"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A reusable quantile reducer factory"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treeLayout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tidy-tree node and link rows in semantic coordinates"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Settled nodes, resolved links, and padded x/y domains"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Granular entry points are:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/bin"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/bin-time"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/bin-xy"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/cumulative"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/fold"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/group"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/mosaic"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/normalize"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/rank"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/reduce"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/select"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/stack"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/waterfall"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/transform/rolling-window"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/box"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/regression"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/network/force"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric, two-dimensional, and calendar bins are separate so specialized\nbinning does not enlarge an ordinary histogram."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"thresholds"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts a count, complete boundary array, or a\nD3-compatible threshold callback such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"thresholdScott"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Collection transforms use action names such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fold"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollingWindow"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"},{"type":"text","value":". A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*RowsX"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*RowsY"},{"type":"text","value":" name is reserved for\nprepared rows paired with a same-named mark family, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"boxRows"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"linearRegressionRowsX/Y"},{"type":"text","value":". Reducers remain scoped to the reduce entry and use\nanalytical names such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delta"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"fold-wide-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fold wide rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fold"},{"type":"text","value":" turns selected fields into long-form rows while preserving the other\nsource fields:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\n\nconst points = fold(rows, {\n  fields: ['R90_10_1980', 'R90_10_2015'] as const,\n  as: { key: 'periodField', value: 'inequality' },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Output is source-row-major, then follows authored "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fields"},{"type":"text","value":" order. Each point\nretains the source row's other properties and adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"periodField"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inequality"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":". Omitting "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"as"},{"type":"text","value":" uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":". Values are\nnot filtered, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" remain available to subsequent\ntransforms or application logic."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a literal field tuple so the output key and value remain correlated when\nTypeScript narrows the key. Duplicate fields, identical output names, and the\nreserved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":" output names throw synchronously with a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fold:"},{"type":"text","value":" error. Output names may replace source fields; lineage retains the\noriginal row."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"group-fields","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group fields"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"by: 'region'"},{"type":"text","value":" preserves the field name in the result. Compound groups use a\nnamed object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const daily = groupBy(orders, {\n  by: {\n    region: 'region',\n    day: (datum) => utcDay.floor(datum.createdAt),\n  },\n  outputs: {\n    revenue: { value: 'amount', reduce: 'sum' },\n    orders: { reduce: 'count' },\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The result contains "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"region"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"day"},{"type":"text","value":", not an opaque "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"},{"type":"text","value":" or tuple."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reducers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reducers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every output names its reducer. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"count"},{"type":"text","value":" omits "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":"; numeric reducers require\nit. Compact built-in strings are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"count"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sum"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mean"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"min"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"max"},{"type":"text","value":".\nTree-shakeable reducer functions provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"median"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"variance"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deviation"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"first"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"last"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delta"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ratio"},{"type":"text","value":" without adding them to every\naggregation bundle."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom reducers receive one object with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"values"},{"type":"text","value":", selected "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", source\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"indexes"},{"type":"text","value":", and the named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group"},{"type":"text","value":" object. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"quantile(probability)"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a\ncustom reducer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Empty "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"count"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sum"},{"type":"text","value":" results are zero. Other empty numeric results are\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"variance"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deviation"},{"type":"text","value":" use the sample denominator and return "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor fewer than two finite values. If a singleton group needs a zero-width\ninterval, state that policy in a small authored reducer."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Numeric reducers ignore non-finite channel values, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":", and a custom reducer's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":" still describe every input row\nin the group. Filter invalid observations before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":" when lineage should\ncontain only contributors."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ordering-and-flat-rows","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ordering and flat rows"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rollingWindow"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cumulative"},{"type":"text","value":" accept "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":" and ascending or descending "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"order"},{"type":"text","value":".\nInput order is used when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rank"},{"type":"text","value":" orders by its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":" and\nsupports competition, dense, and ordinal ties."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One-to-one transforms spread the input row and add named outputs:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const trends = rollingWindow(daily, {\n  by: 'region',\n  orderBy: 'day',\n  size: 28,\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: {\n    revenue28d: { value: 'revenue', reduce: 'sum' },\n    averageOrder28d: { value: 'averageOrder', reduce: 'mean' },\n  },\n})\n\nlineY(trends, { x: 'day', y: 'revenue28d', color: 'region' })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There is no nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"datum.datum"},{"type":"text","value":" path. A named output may intentionally replace\nan input field; the original rows remain available through lineage. Structural\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":" names are reserved."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"mosaic-intervals","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mosaic intervals"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mosaicY"},{"type":"text","value":" allocates outer category totals across x, then normalizes y values\nwithin each x category. Keep aggregation explicit so the definition shows\nwhether a cell represents a count or a weighted sum:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { groupBy, mosaicY, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst counts = groupBy(responses, {\n  by: { question: 'question', response: 'response' },\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n})\n\nconst cells = mosaicY(counts, {\n  x: 'question',\n  y: 'response',\n  value: 'count',\n  yOrder: ['Strongly disagree', 'Disagree', 'Neutral', 'Agree'],\n})\n\nrect(cells, {\n  x: 'x',\n  x1: 'x1',\n  x2: 'x2',\n  y: 'y',\n  y1: 'y1',\n  y2: 'y2',\n  color: 'yValue',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each row retains the aggregate input fields and adds semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xValue"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yValue"},{"type":"text","value":", normalized centers and endpoints, the cell "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"value"},{"type":"text","value":", its outer-group\ntotal, the grand total, and direct lineage. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mosaicX"},{"type":"text","value":" transposes the policy:\ny-category totals determine row heights and x values compose within each row.\nUse "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" to make categorical ordering explicit. Duplicate\nx/y pairs throw; aggregate them first with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"groupBy"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"waterfall-intervals","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Waterfall intervals"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"waterfall"},{"type":"text","value":" turns signed contributions into ordered cumulative intervals. It\ndoes not derive the contributions themselves, so analytical intent remains\nvisible beside the chart:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { barY, delta, rollingWindow } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { waterfall } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/waterfall'\n\nconst changes = rollingWindow(observations, {\n  orderBy: 'year',\n  size: 2,\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: { delta: { value: 'price', reduce: delta } },\n})\n\nconst bridge = waterfall(changes, {\n  value: 'delta',\n  orderBy: 'year',\n  total: true,\n})\n\nbarY(bridge, {\n  x: (row) => (row.kind === 'total' ? 'Total' : row.year),\n  y1: 'start',\n  y2: 'end',\n  color: 'kind',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each valid step retains the input row, adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delta"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"},{"type":"text","value":", and an\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"increase"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"decrease"},{"type":"text","value":" kind, and carries one-row direct lineage. Zero is an\nincrease so it remains available to downstream policy. Nullish and nonfinite\nvalues are omitted. A nonfinite cumulative result throws instead of emitting\ninvalid geometry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"total: true"},{"type":"text","value":" appends one zero-based "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"total"},{"type":"text","value":" row for every nonempty group. The\ntotal is a discriminated synthetic row containing only group fields, derived\nfields, and aggregate direct lineage; it does not clone an arbitrary last\nsource row. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"by"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orderBy"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"order"},{"type":"text","value":" use the same first-seen grouping and\nstable ordering contracts as the other eager transforms. Group output names\ncannot collide with waterfall or lineage fields."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tidy-hierarchy-trees","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tidy hierarchy trees"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treeLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" turns flat path or parent-reference rows into positioned nodes and\nlinks. Import it from its exact optional entry:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\n\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  path: 'name',\n  delimiter: '.',\n  orientation: 'left',\n  nodeSize: [1, 1],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"path"},{"type":"text","value":" and an optional one-character "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"delimiter"},{"type":"text","value":" for full semantic paths.\nMissing path ancestors are imputed with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data: null"},{"type":"text","value":" and empty lineage. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentId"},{"type":"text","value":" instead when every node is an explicit row:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  id: 'id',\n  parentId: 'parentId',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The two input forms are mutually exclusive. IDs must be unique, every\nnon-root parent must exist, and the rows must form one acyclic hierarchy.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"separation"},{"type":"text","value":" receive immutable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeNodeContext"},{"type":"text","value":" objects with\nidentity, raw data, depth, height, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"internal"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"external"},{"type":"text","value":" flags, and lineage. Input\norder remains the child order when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sort"},{"type":"text","value":" is omitted."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Path-mode IDs use canonical slash form and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" is the terminal path segment.\nExplicit-parent IDs are opaque, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" is the complete authored ID even\nwhen it contains a slash."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Output nodes contain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"parentId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":", nullable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"depth"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"height"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"internal"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"external"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":".\nEach link contains stable source and target IDs, resolved endpoint nodes and\nindexes, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":", and the target node's raw-row lineage. A link\nuses its target ID as its own ID because each non-root tree node has one\nincoming link. Endpoint indexes are null when the corresponding path ancestor\nwas imputed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"orientation"},{"type":"text","value":" selects the root anchor: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"left"},{"type":"text","value":" is the default, with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"right"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bottom"},{"type":"text","value":" also available. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodeSize"},{"type":"text","value":" is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[breadth, depth]"},{"type":"text","value":" in semantic\ndata-space units. Normal positional scales own responsive projection, so this\neager transform does not depend on final chart bounds. It is uncached; memoize\nunchanged hierarchy input with other derived data."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"static-force-layouts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static force layouts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" runs a stopped D3 force simulation synchronously and returns\nordinary rows for native marks. Import it from its exact optional entry:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { forceLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/network/force'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  iterations: 300,\n  domainPadding: 0.2,\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'link', distance: 42 },\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -120 },\n    { type: 'center', x: 0, y: 0 },\n    { type: 'collide', radius: 9, strength: 0.9 },\n    { type: 'x', x: 0, strength: 0.03 },\n    { type: 'y', y: 0, strength: 0.03 },\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The built-in force descriptors are explicit and applied in authored order.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"link"},{"type":"text","value":" accepts link-row distance and strength channels. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"manyBody.strength"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collide.radius"},{"type":"text","value":", and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":" targets and strengths accept node-row\nchannels. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"center"},{"type":"text","value":" coordinates and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"collide.strength"},{"type":"text","value":" are fixed values. Each\nbuilt-in force type may appear at most once."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Named custom factories accept any native D3-compatible force:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { forceRadial } from 'd3-force'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -80 },\n    {\n      type: 'custom',\n      name: 'radial',\n      create: () => forceRadial(120, 0, 0).strength(0.08),\n    },\n  ],\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"create"},{"type":"text","value":" receives one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceFactoryContext"},{"type":"text","value":" with the private mutable node and\nlink clones, immutable resolved endpoint-key arrays, and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"nodeKey"},{"type":"text","value":" accessor\ncompatible with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3.forceLink().id(...)"},{"type":"text","value":". The factory must return a D3 "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Force"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe working-clone types reserve D3's simulation fields; valid numeric node\nseeds are retained, while conflicting source fields cannot leak into D3 state.\nNames are nonempty and unique across custom and built-in forces. Factories may\nconfigure or close over the private records, but must not add, remove, or\nreorder them. The transform validates collection identity and final finite\ncoordinates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The transform clones its inputs before D3 mutates simulation state. Output\nnodes retain non-reserved source fields and add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vx"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"vy"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":". Output links retain their raw endpoint keys and add\nresolved node references, source and target indexes, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x2"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y2"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceRows"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":". The result also contains "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xDomain"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"yDomain"},{"type":"text","value":" for configured positional scales."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Node keys must be unique, and every link endpoint must match one. Stable input\nand deterministic force factories produce repeatable static settlement. The\nsimulation remains stopped and ticks synchronously; a custom force does not\ngain a timer or live-state ownership. The transform is eager, chart-size\nindependent, and uncached; memoize it with other derived data when a framework\ncomponent rebuilds unchanged input."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This API does not run a live simulation or own drag state. Products that need\ncontinuous physics or node dragging should keep that controller and its\npositions in application state, then render the current rows through normal\nmarks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"lineage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lineage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Aggregations expose "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"source"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sourceIndexes"},{"type":"text","value":". Row-extending transforms\nexpose the source rows used for that derived value. This supports inspection,\ntooltips, drill-down, and subsequent transforms without renderer knowledge."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lineage is direct to the immediate input. In a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fold"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"},{"type":"text","value":" pipeline,\nthe normalized row points to its folded input, and that folded row points to\nthe original source row. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the chosen input rows unchanged."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Transforms and Reactivity"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for\ncomposition and memoization guidance."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Value and grouping contracts are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformAccessor"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformAccessorContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformField"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformValue"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformValueOutput"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformKey"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformGroupSpec"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformGroupRow"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformOrder"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformOrderOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformLineage"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reducer contracts are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformNumericReducer"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformReducer"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformReduceContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformOutputSpec"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformOutputs"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformOutputValue"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TransformOutputRow"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GroupByOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GroupByDatum"},{"type":"text","value":". Numeric bin exports are\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinXDatum"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinYDatum"},{"type":"text","value":". Two-dimensional bin exports are\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinXYOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinXYDatum"},{"type":"text","value":". Calendar bin exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TimeIntervalLike"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinTimeOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"BinTimeDatum"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fold exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FoldField"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FoldOutputNames"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FoldOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"FoldDatum"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rolling exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RollingWindowOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RollingWindowDatum"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RollingWindowAnchor"},{"type":"text","value":".\nCumulative exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CumulativeOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CumulativeDatum"},{"type":"text","value":". Rank exports\nare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RankOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RankDatum"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RankTies"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Normalization exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NormalizeOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NormalizeDatum"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NormalizeBasis"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NormalizeContext"},{"type":"text","value":". Selection exports are\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SelectOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SelectMethod"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SelectContext"},{"type":"text","value":". Row-stack exports are\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StackRowsXOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StackRowsXDatum"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StackRowsYOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"StackRowsYDatum"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mosaic exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MosaicOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MosaicXDatum"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MosaicYDatum"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Waterfall exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WaterfallKind"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WaterfallOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WaterfallDatum"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WaterfallStepDatum"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"WaterfallTotalDatum"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static force-layout exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"forceLayout"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLayoutOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceNumericValue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLinkDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceManyBodyDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceCenterDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceCollideDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceXDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceYDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceFactoryDescriptor"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceFactory"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceFactoryContext"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLayoutWorkingNode"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLayoutWorkingLink"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLayoutResult"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLayoutNode"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLayoutLink"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ForceLinkLineage"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tidy-tree exports are "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"treeLayout"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeOrientation"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeNodeContext"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeNodeComparator"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeNodeSeparation"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeLayoutPathOptions"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeLayoutParentOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeLayoutOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeLayoutNode"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeLayoutLink"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TreeLayoutResult"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"functions","text":"Functions","level":2},{"id":"fold-wide-rows","text":"Fold wide rows","level":2},{"id":"group-fields","text":"Group fields","level":2},{"id":"reducers","text":"Reducers","level":2},{"id":"ordering-and-flat-rows","text":"Ordering and flat rows","level":2},{"id":"mosaic-intervals","text":"Mosaic intervals","level":2},{"id":"waterfall-intervals","text":"Waterfall intervals","level":2},{"id":"tidy-hierarchy-trees","text":"Tidy hierarchy trees","level":2},{"id":"static-force-layouts","text":"Static force layouts","level":2},{"id":"lineage","text":"Lineage","level":2},{"id":"types","text":"Types","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTransforms are ordinary functions: source rows in, typed derived data out.\nThey do not rewrite mark options, retain state, cache results, or own framework\nreactivity.\n\n## Functions\n\n| Export                     | Result                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `fold`                     | Wide rows repeated into authored field/value pairs       |\n| `groupBy`                  | Named group fields, reducer outputs, and lineage         |\n| `binX`, `binY`             | Numeric intervals on one axis                            |\n| `binXY`                    | Numeric cells with x and y intervals                     |\n| `binTimeX`, `binTimeY`     | Calendar-aligned intervals from a supplied time interval |\n| `rollingWindow`            | Flat input rows extended with rolling outputs            |\n| `cumulative`               | Flat input rows extended with running outputs            |\n| `rank`                     | Flat input rows extended with ranks                      |\n| `normalize`                | Flat input rows extended with normalized values          |\n| `select`                   | Selected original rows                                   |\n| `stackRowsX`, `stackRowsY` | Flat input rows extended with stack endpoints            |\n| `mosaicX`, `mosaicY`       | Two normalized proportional interval dimensions          |\n| `boxRows`                  | Tukey summary and outlier rows by category               |\n| `linearRegressionRowsX/Y`  | Sampled least-squares fits and confidence bounds         |\n| `waterfall`                | Ordered signed contributions as cumulative intervals     |\n| `quantile`                 | A reusable quantile reducer factory                      |\n| `treeLayout`               | Tidy-tree node and link rows in semantic coordinates     |\n| `forceLayout`              | Settled nodes, resolved links, and padded x/y domains    |\n\nGranular entry points are:\n\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/bin`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/bin-time`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/bin-xy`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/cumulative`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/fold`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/group`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/mosaic`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/normalize`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/rank`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/reduce`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/select`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/stack`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/waterfall`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/rolling-window`\n- `@tanstack/charts/box`\n- `@tanstack/charts/regression`\n- `@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree`\n- `@tanstack/charts/network/force`\n\nNumeric, two-dimensional, and calendar bins are separate so specialized\nbinning does not enlarge an ordinary histogram.\n\nNumeric `thresholds` accepts a count, complete boundary array, or a\nD3-compatible threshold callback such as `thresholdScott`.\n\nCollection transforms use action names such as `fold`, `groupBy`,\n`rollingWindow`, and `normalize`. A `*RowsX` or `*RowsY` name is reserved for\nprepared rows paired with a same-named mark family, such as `boxRows` and\n`linearRegressionRowsX/Y`. Reducers remain scoped to the reduce entry and use\nanalytical names such as `delta`.\n\n## Fold wide rows\n\n`fold` turns selected fields into long-form rows while preserving the other\nsource fields:\n\n```ts\nimport { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\n\nconst points = fold(rows, {\n  fields: ['R90_10_1980', 'R90_10_2015'] as const,\n  as: { key: 'periodField', value: 'inequality' },\n})\n```\n\nOutput is source-row-major, then follows authored `fields` order. Each point\nretains the source row's other properties and adds `periodField`, `inequality`,\n`source`, and `sourceIndexes`. Omitting `as` uses `key` and `value`. Values are\nnot filtered, so `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN` remain available to subsequent\ntransforms or application logic.\n\nUse a literal field tuple so the output key and value remain correlated when\nTypeScript narrows the key. Duplicate fields, identical output names, and the\nreserved `source` or `sourceIndexes` output names throw synchronously with a\n`fold:` error. Output names may replace source fields; lineage retains the\noriginal row.\n\n## Group fields\n\n`by: 'region'` preserves the field name in the result. Compound groups use a\nnamed object:\n\n```ts\nconst daily = groupBy(orders, {\n  by: {\n    region: 'region',\n    day: (datum) => utcDay.floor(datum.createdAt),\n  },\n  outputs: {\n    revenue: { value: 'amount', reduce: 'sum' },\n    orders: { reduce: 'count' },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe result contains `region` and `day`, not an opaque `key` or tuple.\n\n## Reducers\n\nEvery output names its reducer. `count` omits `value`; numeric reducers require\nit. Compact built-in strings are `count`, `sum`, `mean`, `min`, and `max`.\nTree-shakeable reducer functions provide `median`, `variance`, `deviation`,\n`first`, `last`, `delta`, and `ratio` without adding them to every\naggregation bundle.\n\nCustom reducers receive one object with `values`, selected `data`, source\n`indexes`, and the named `group` object. `quantile(probability)` returns a\ncustom reducer.\n\nEmpty `count` and `sum` results are zero. Other empty numeric results are\n`NaN`. `variance` and `deviation` use the sample denominator and return `NaN`\nfor fewer than two finite values. If a singleton group needs a zero-width\ninterval, state that policy in a small authored reducer.\n\nNumeric reducers ignore non-finite channel values, while `source`,\n`sourceIndexes`, and a custom reducer's `data` still describe every input row\nin the group. Filter invalid observations before `groupBy` when lineage should\ncontain only contributors.\n\n## Ordering and flat rows\n\n`rollingWindow` and `cumulative` accept `orderBy` and ascending or descending `order`.\nInput order is used when `orderBy` is omitted. `rank` orders by its `value` and\nsupports competition, dense, and ordinal ties.\n\nOne-to-one transforms spread the input row and add named outputs:\n\n```ts\nconst trends = rollingWindow(daily, {\n  by: 'region',\n  orderBy: 'day',\n  size: 28,\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: {\n    revenue28d: { value: 'revenue', reduce: 'sum' },\n    averageOrder28d: { value: 'averageOrder', reduce: 'mean' },\n  },\n})\n\nlineY(trends, { x: 'day', y: 'revenue28d', color: 'region' })\n```\n\nThere is no nested `datum.datum` path. A named output may intentionally replace\nan input field; the original rows remain available through lineage. Structural\n`source` and `sourceIndexes` names are reserved.\n\n## Mosaic intervals\n\n`mosaicY` allocates outer category totals across x, then normalizes y values\nwithin each x category. Keep aggregation explicit so the definition shows\nwhether a cell represents a count or a weighted sum:\n\n```ts\nimport { groupBy, mosaicY, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst counts = groupBy(responses, {\n  by: { question: 'question', response: 'response' },\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n})\n\nconst cells = mosaicY(counts, {\n  x: 'question',\n  y: 'response',\n  value: 'count',\n  yOrder: ['Strongly disagree', 'Disagree', 'Neutral', 'Agree'],\n})\n\nrect(cells, {\n  x: 'x',\n  x1: 'x1',\n  x2: 'x2',\n  y: 'y',\n  y1: 'y1',\n  y2: 'y2',\n  color: 'yValue',\n})\n```\n\nEach row retains the aggregate input fields and adds semantic `xValue` and\n`yValue`, normalized centers and endpoints, the cell `value`, its outer-group\ntotal, the grand total, and direct lineage. `mosaicX` transposes the policy:\ny-category totals determine row heights and x values compose within each row.\nUse `xOrder` and `yOrder` to make categorical ordering explicit. Duplicate\nx/y pairs throw; aggregate them first with `groupBy`.\n\n## Waterfall intervals\n\n`waterfall` turns signed contributions into ordered cumulative intervals. It\ndoes not derive the contributions themselves, so analytical intent remains\nvisible beside the chart:\n\n```ts\nimport { barY, delta, rollingWindow } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { waterfall } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/waterfall'\n\nconst changes = rollingWindow(observations, {\n  orderBy: 'year',\n  size: 2,\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: { delta: { value: 'price', reduce: delta } },\n})\n\nconst bridge = waterfall(changes, {\n  value: 'delta',\n  orderBy: 'year',\n  total: true,\n})\n\nbarY(bridge, {\n  x: (row) => (row.kind === 'total' ? 'Total' : row.year),\n  y1: 'start',\n  y2: 'end',\n  color: 'kind',\n})\n```\n\nEach valid step retains the input row, adds `delta`, `start`, `end`, and an\n`increase` or `decrease` kind, and carries one-row direct lineage. Zero is an\nincrease so it remains available to downstream policy. Nullish and nonfinite\nvalues are omitted. A nonfinite cumulative result throws instead of emitting\ninvalid geometry.\n\n`total: true` appends one zero-based `total` row for every nonempty group. The\ntotal is a discriminated synthetic row containing only group fields, derived\nfields, and aggregate direct lineage; it does not clone an arbitrary last\nsource row. `by`, `orderBy`, and `order` use the same first-seen grouping and\nstable ordering contracts as the other eager transforms. Group output names\ncannot collide with waterfall or lineage fields.\n\n## Tidy hierarchy trees\n\n`treeLayout` turns flat path or parent-reference rows into positioned nodes and\nlinks. Import it from its exact optional entry:\n\n```ts\nimport { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\n\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  path: 'name',\n  delimiter: '.',\n  orientation: 'left',\n  nodeSize: [1, 1],\n})\n```\n\nUse `path` and an optional one-character `delimiter` for full semantic paths.\nMissing path ancestors are imputed with `data: null` and empty lineage. Use\n`id` and `parentId` instead when every node is an explicit row:\n\n```ts\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  id: 'id',\n  parentId: 'parentId',\n})\n```\n\nThe two input forms are mutually exclusive. IDs must be unique, every\nnon-root parent must exist, and the rows must form one acyclic hierarchy.\n`sort` and `separation` receive immutable `TreeNodeContext` objects with\nidentity, raw data, depth, height, `internal`/`external` flags, and lineage. Input\norder remains the child order when `sort` is omitted.\n\nPath-mode IDs use canonical slash form and `name` is the terminal path segment.\nExplicit-parent IDs are opaque, so `name` is the complete authored ID even\nwhen it contains a slash.\n\nOutput nodes contain `id`, `parentId`, `name`, nullable `data`, `depth`,\n`height`, `internal`, `external`, `x`, `y`, `source`, and `sourceIndexes`.\nEach link contains stable source and target IDs, resolved endpoint nodes and\nindexes, `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`, and the target node's raw-row lineage. A link\nuses its target ID as its own ID because each non-root tree node has one\nincoming link. Endpoint indexes are null when the corresponding path ancestor\nwas imputed.\n\n`orientation` selects the root anchor: `left` is the default, with `right`,\n`top`, and `bottom` also available. `nodeSize` is `[breadth, depth]` in semantic\ndata-space units. Normal positional scales own responsive projection, so this\neager transform does not depend on final chart bounds. It is uncached; memoize\nunchanged hierarchy input with other derived data.\n\n## Static force layouts\n\n`forceLayout` runs a stopped D3 force simulation synchronously and returns\nordinary rows for native marks. Import it from its exact optional entry:\n\n```ts\nimport { forceLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/network/force'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  iterations: 300,\n  domainPadding: 0.2,\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'link', distance: 42 },\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -120 },\n    { type: 'center', x: 0, y: 0 },\n    { type: 'collide', radius: 9, strength: 0.9 },\n    { type: 'x', x: 0, strength: 0.03 },\n    { type: 'y', y: 0, strength: 0.03 },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nThe built-in force descriptors are explicit and applied in authored order.\n`link` accepts link-row distance and strength channels. `manyBody.strength`,\n`collide.radius`, and the `x` and `y` targets and strengths accept node-row\nchannels. `center` coordinates and `collide.strength` are fixed values. Each\nbuilt-in force type may appear at most once.\n\nNamed custom factories accept any native D3-compatible force:\n\n```ts\nimport { forceRadial } from 'd3-force'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -80 },\n    {\n      type: 'custom',\n      name: 'radial',\n      create: () => forceRadial(120, 0, 0).strength(0.08),\n    },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`create` receives one `ForceFactoryContext` with the private mutable node and\nlink clones, immutable resolved endpoint-key arrays, and a `nodeKey` accessor\ncompatible with `d3.forceLink().id(...)`. The factory must return a D3 `Force`.\nThe working-clone types reserve D3's simulation fields; valid numeric node\nseeds are retained, while conflicting source fields cannot leak into D3 state.\nNames are nonempty and unique across custom and built-in forces. Factories may\nconfigure or close over the private records, but must not add, remove, or\nreorder them. The transform validates collection identity and final finite\ncoordinates.\n\nThe transform clones its inputs before D3 mutates simulation state. Output\nnodes retain non-reserved source fields and add `x`, `y`, `vx`, `vy`, `source`,\nand `sourceIndexes`. Output links retain their raw endpoint keys and add\nresolved node references, source and target indexes, `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`,\n`sourceRows`, and `sourceIndexes`. The result also contains `xDomain` and\n`yDomain` for configured positional scales.\n\nNode keys must be unique, and every link endpoint must match one. Stable input\nand deterministic force factories produce repeatable static settlement. The\nsimulation remains stopped and ticks synchronously; a custom force does not\ngain a timer or live-state ownership. The transform is eager, chart-size\nindependent, and uncached; memoize it with other derived data when a framework\ncomponent rebuilds unchanged input.\n\nThis API does not run a live simulation or own drag state. Products that need\ncontinuous physics or node dragging should keep that controller and its\npositions in application state, then render the current rows through normal\nmarks.\n\n## Lineage\n\nAggregations expose `source` and `sourceIndexes`. Row-extending transforms\nexpose the source rows used for that derived value. This supports inspection,\ntooltips, drill-down, and subsequent transforms without renderer knowledge.\n\nLineage is direct to the immediate input. In a `fold` → `normalize` pipeline,\nthe normalized row points to its folded input, and that folded row points to\nthe original source row. `select` returns the chosen input rows unchanged.\n\nSee [Transforms and Reactivity](../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md) for\ncomposition and memoization guidance.\n\n## Types\n\nValue and grouping contracts are `TransformAccessor`,\n`TransformAccessorContext`, `TransformField`, `TransformValue`,\n`TransformValueOutput`, `TransformKey`, `TransformGroupSpec`,\n`TransformGroupRow`, `TransformOrder`, `TransformOrderOptions`, and\n`TransformLineage`.\n\nReducer contracts are `TransformNumericReducer`, `TransformReducer`,\n`TransformReduceContext`, `TransformOutputSpec`, `TransformOutputs`,\n`TransformOutputValue`, and `TransformOutputRow`.\n\nGroup exports are `GroupByOptions` and `GroupByDatum`. Numeric bin exports are\n`BinOptions`, `BinXDatum`, and `BinYDatum`. Two-dimensional bin exports are\n`BinXYOptions` and `BinXYDatum`. Calendar bin exports are `TimeIntervalLike`,\n`BinTimeOptions`, and `BinTimeDatum`.\n\nFold exports are `FoldField`, `FoldOutputNames`, `FoldOptions`, and\n`FoldDatum`.\n\nRolling exports are `RollingWindowOptions`, `RollingWindowDatum`, and `RollingWindowAnchor`.\nCumulative exports are `CumulativeOptions` and `CumulativeDatum`. Rank exports\nare `RankOptions`, `RankDatum`, and `RankTies`.\n\nNormalization exports are `NormalizeOptions`, `NormalizeDatum`,\n`NormalizeBasis`, and `NormalizeContext`. Selection exports are\n`SelectOptions`, `SelectMethod`, and `SelectContext`. Row-stack exports are\n`StackRowsXOptions`, `StackRowsXDatum`, `StackRowsYOptions`, and\n`StackRowsYDatum`.\n\nMosaic exports are `MosaicOptions`, `MosaicXDatum`, and `MosaicYDatum`.\n\nWaterfall exports are `WaterfallKind`, `WaterfallOptions`, `WaterfallDatum`,\n`WaterfallStepDatum`, and `WaterfallTotalDatum`.\n\nStatic force-layout exports are `forceLayout`, `ForceLayoutOptions`,\n`ForceDescriptor`, `ForceNumericValue`, `ForceLinkDescriptor`,\n`ForceManyBodyDescriptor`, `ForceCenterDescriptor`, `ForceCollideDescriptor`,\n`ForceXDescriptor`, `ForceYDescriptor`, `ForceFactoryDescriptor`,\n`ForceFactory`, `ForceFactoryContext`, `ForceLayoutWorkingNode`,\n`ForceLayoutWorkingLink`, `ForceLayoutResult`, `ForceLayoutNode`,\n`ForceLayoutLink`, and `ForceLinkLineage`.\n\nTidy-tree exports are `treeLayout`, `TreeOrientation`, `TreeNodeContext`,\n`TreeNodeComparator`, `TreeNodeSeparation`, `TreeLayoutPathOptions`,\n`TreeLayoutParentOptions`, `TreeLayoutOptions`, `TreeLayoutNode`,\n`TreeLayoutLink`, and `TreeLayoutResult`.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Data Transforms\ndescription: Typed, eager transforms for reusable application data.\n---\n\nTransforms are ordinary functions: source rows in, typed derived data out.\nThey do not rewrite mark options, retain state, cache results, or own framework\nreactivity.\n\n## Functions\n\n| Export                     | Result                                                   |\n| -------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `fold`                     | Wide rows repeated into authored field/value pairs       |\n| `groupBy`                  | Named group fields, reducer outputs, and lineage         |\n| `binX`, `binY`             | Numeric intervals on one axis                            |\n| `binXY`                    | Numeric cells with x and y intervals                     |\n| `binTimeX`, `binTimeY`     | Calendar-aligned intervals from a supplied time interval |\n| `rollingWindow`            | Flat input rows extended with rolling outputs            |\n| `cumulative`               | Flat input rows extended with running outputs            |\n| `rank`                     | Flat input rows extended with ranks                      |\n| `normalize`                | Flat input rows extended with normalized values          |\n| `select`                   | Selected original rows                                   |\n| `stackRowsX`, `stackRowsY` | Flat input rows extended with stack endpoints            |\n| `mosaicX`, `mosaicY`       | Two normalized proportional interval dimensions          |\n| `boxRows`                  | Tukey summary and outlier rows by category               |\n| `linearRegressionRowsX/Y`  | Sampled least-squares fits and confidence bounds         |\n| `waterfall`                | Ordered signed contributions as cumulative intervals     |\n| `quantile`                 | A reusable quantile reducer factory                      |\n| `treeLayout`               | Tidy-tree node and link rows in semantic coordinates     |\n| `forceLayout`              | Settled nodes, resolved links, and padded x/y domains    |\n\nGranular entry points are:\n\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/bin`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/bin-time`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/bin-xy`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/cumulative`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/fold`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/group`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/mosaic`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/normalize`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/rank`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/reduce`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/select`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/stack`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/waterfall`\n- `@tanstack/charts/transform/rolling-window`\n- `@tanstack/charts/box`\n- `@tanstack/charts/regression`\n- `@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree`\n- `@tanstack/charts/network/force`\n\nNumeric, two-dimensional, and calendar bins are separate so specialized\nbinning does not enlarge an ordinary histogram.\n\nNumeric `thresholds` accepts a count, complete boundary array, or a\nD3-compatible threshold callback such as `thresholdScott`.\n\nCollection transforms use action names such as `fold`, `groupBy`,\n`rollingWindow`, and `normalize`. A `*RowsX` or `*RowsY` name is reserved for\nprepared rows paired with a same-named mark family, such as `boxRows` and\n`linearRegressionRowsX/Y`. Reducers remain scoped to the reduce entry and use\nanalytical names such as `delta`.\n\n## Fold wide rows\n\n`fold` turns selected fields into long-form rows while preserving the other\nsource fields:\n\n```ts\nimport { fold } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/fold'\n\nconst points = fold(rows, {\n  fields: ['R90_10_1980', 'R90_10_2015'] as const,\n  as: { key: 'periodField', value: 'inequality' },\n})\n```\n\nOutput is source-row-major, then follows authored `fields` order. Each point\nretains the source row's other properties and adds `periodField`, `inequality`,\n`source`, and `sourceIndexes`. Omitting `as` uses `key` and `value`. Values are\nnot filtered, so `null`, `undefined`, and `NaN` remain available to subsequent\ntransforms or application logic.\n\nUse a literal field tuple so the output key and value remain correlated when\nTypeScript narrows the key. Duplicate fields, identical output names, and the\nreserved `source` or `sourceIndexes` output names throw synchronously with a\n`fold:` error. Output names may replace source fields; lineage retains the\noriginal row.\n\n## Group fields\n\n`by: 'region'` preserves the field name in the result. Compound groups use a\nnamed object:\n\n```ts\nconst daily = groupBy(orders, {\n  by: {\n    region: 'region',\n    day: (datum) => utcDay.floor(datum.createdAt),\n  },\n  outputs: {\n    revenue: { value: 'amount', reduce: 'sum' },\n    orders: { reduce: 'count' },\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThe result contains `region` and `day`, not an opaque `key` or tuple.\n\n## Reducers\n\nEvery output names its reducer. `count` omits `value`; numeric reducers require\nit. Compact built-in strings are `count`, `sum`, `mean`, `min`, and `max`.\nTree-shakeable reducer functions provide `median`, `variance`, `deviation`,\n`first`, `last`, `delta`, and `ratio` without adding them to every\naggregation bundle.\n\nCustom reducers receive one object with `values`, selected `data`, source\n`indexes`, and the named `group` object. `quantile(probability)` returns a\ncustom reducer.\n\nEmpty `count` and `sum` results are zero. Other empty numeric results are\n`NaN`. `variance` and `deviation` use the sample denominator and return `NaN`\nfor fewer than two finite values. If a singleton group needs a zero-width\ninterval, state that policy in a small authored reducer.\n\nNumeric reducers ignore non-finite channel values, while `source`,\n`sourceIndexes`, and a custom reducer's `data` still describe every input row\nin the group. Filter invalid observations before `groupBy` when lineage should\ncontain only contributors.\n\n## Ordering and flat rows\n\n`rollingWindow` and `cumulative` accept `orderBy` and ascending or descending `order`.\nInput order is used when `orderBy` is omitted. `rank` orders by its `value` and\nsupports competition, dense, and ordinal ties.\n\nOne-to-one transforms spread the input row and add named outputs:\n\n```ts\nconst trends = rollingWindow(daily, {\n  by: 'region',\n  orderBy: 'day',\n  size: 28,\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: {\n    revenue28d: { value: 'revenue', reduce: 'sum' },\n    averageOrder28d: { value: 'averageOrder', reduce: 'mean' },\n  },\n})\n\nlineY(trends, { x: 'day', y: 'revenue28d', color: 'region' })\n```\n\nThere is no nested `datum.datum` path. A named output may intentionally replace\nan input field; the original rows remain available through lineage. Structural\n`source` and `sourceIndexes` names are reserved.\n\n## Mosaic intervals\n\n`mosaicY` allocates outer category totals across x, then normalizes y values\nwithin each x category. Keep aggregation explicit so the definition shows\nwhether a cell represents a count or a weighted sum:\n\n```ts\nimport { groupBy, mosaicY, rect } from '@tanstack/charts'\n\nconst counts = groupBy(responses, {\n  by: { question: 'question', response: 'response' },\n  outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n})\n\nconst cells = mosaicY(counts, {\n  x: 'question',\n  y: 'response',\n  value: 'count',\n  yOrder: ['Strongly disagree', 'Disagree', 'Neutral', 'Agree'],\n})\n\nrect(cells, {\n  x: 'x',\n  x1: 'x1',\n  x2: 'x2',\n  y: 'y',\n  y1: 'y1',\n  y2: 'y2',\n  color: 'yValue',\n})\n```\n\nEach row retains the aggregate input fields and adds semantic `xValue` and\n`yValue`, normalized centers and endpoints, the cell `value`, its outer-group\ntotal, the grand total, and direct lineage. `mosaicX` transposes the policy:\ny-category totals determine row heights and x values compose within each row.\nUse `xOrder` and `yOrder` to make categorical ordering explicit. Duplicate\nx/y pairs throw; aggregate them first with `groupBy`.\n\n## Waterfall intervals\n\n`waterfall` turns signed contributions into ordered cumulative intervals. It\ndoes not derive the contributions themselves, so analytical intent remains\nvisible beside the chart:\n\n```ts\nimport { barY, delta, rollingWindow } from '@tanstack/charts'\nimport { waterfall } from '@tanstack/charts/transform/waterfall'\n\nconst changes = rollingWindow(observations, {\n  orderBy: 'year',\n  size: 2,\n  partial: false,\n  outputs: { delta: { value: 'price', reduce: delta } },\n})\n\nconst bridge = waterfall(changes, {\n  value: 'delta',\n  orderBy: 'year',\n  total: true,\n})\n\nbarY(bridge, {\n  x: (row) => (row.kind === 'total' ? 'Total' : row.year),\n  y1: 'start',\n  y2: 'end',\n  color: 'kind',\n})\n```\n\nEach valid step retains the input row, adds `delta`, `start`, `end`, and an\n`increase` or `decrease` kind, and carries one-row direct lineage. Zero is an\nincrease so it remains available to downstream policy. Nullish and nonfinite\nvalues are omitted. A nonfinite cumulative result throws instead of emitting\ninvalid geometry.\n\n`total: true` appends one zero-based `total` row for every nonempty group. The\ntotal is a discriminated synthetic row containing only group fields, derived\nfields, and aggregate direct lineage; it does not clone an arbitrary last\nsource row. `by`, `orderBy`, and `order` use the same first-seen grouping and\nstable ordering contracts as the other eager transforms. Group output names\ncannot collide with waterfall or lineage fields.\n\n## Tidy hierarchy trees\n\n`treeLayout` turns flat path or parent-reference rows into positioned nodes and\nlinks. Import it from its exact optional entry:\n\n```ts\nimport { treeLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/hierarchy/tree'\n\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  path: 'name',\n  delimiter: '.',\n  orientation: 'left',\n  nodeSize: [1, 1],\n})\n```\n\nUse `path` and an optional one-character `delimiter` for full semantic paths.\nMissing path ancestors are imputed with `data: null` and empty lineage. Use\n`id` and `parentId` instead when every node is an explicit row:\n\n```ts\nconst hierarchy = treeLayout(rows, {\n  id: 'id',\n  parentId: 'parentId',\n})\n```\n\nThe two input forms are mutually exclusive. IDs must be unique, every\nnon-root parent must exist, and the rows must form one acyclic hierarchy.\n`sort` and `separation` receive immutable `TreeNodeContext` objects with\nidentity, raw data, depth, height, `internal`/`external` flags, and lineage. Input\norder remains the child order when `sort` is omitted.\n\nPath-mode IDs use canonical slash form and `name` is the terminal path segment.\nExplicit-parent IDs are opaque, so `name` is the complete authored ID even\nwhen it contains a slash.\n\nOutput nodes contain `id`, `parentId`, `name`, nullable `data`, `depth`,\n`height`, `internal`, `external`, `x`, `y`, `source`, and `sourceIndexes`.\nEach link contains stable source and target IDs, resolved endpoint nodes and\nindexes, `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`, and the target node's raw-row lineage. A link\nuses its target ID as its own ID because each non-root tree node has one\nincoming link. Endpoint indexes are null when the corresponding path ancestor\nwas imputed.\n\n`orientation` selects the root anchor: `left` is the default, with `right`,\n`top`, and `bottom` also available. `nodeSize` is `[breadth, depth]` in semantic\ndata-space units. Normal positional scales own responsive projection, so this\neager transform does not depend on final chart bounds. It is uncached; memoize\nunchanged hierarchy input with other derived data.\n\n## Static force layouts\n\n`forceLayout` runs a stopped D3 force simulation synchronously and returns\nordinary rows for native marks. Import it from its exact optional entry:\n\n```ts\nimport { forceLayout } from '@tanstack/charts/network/force'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  iterations: 300,\n  domainPadding: 0.2,\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'link', distance: 42 },\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -120 },\n    { type: 'center', x: 0, y: 0 },\n    { type: 'collide', radius: 9, strength: 0.9 },\n    { type: 'x', x: 0, strength: 0.03 },\n    { type: 'y', y: 0, strength: 0.03 },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\nThe built-in force descriptors are explicit and applied in authored order.\n`link` accepts link-row distance and strength channels. `manyBody.strength`,\n`collide.radius`, and the `x` and `y` targets and strengths accept node-row\nchannels. `center` coordinates and `collide.strength` are fixed values. Each\nbuilt-in force type may appear at most once.\n\nNamed custom factories accept any native D3-compatible force:\n\n```ts\nimport { forceRadial } from 'd3-force'\n\nconst graph = forceLayout(nodes, links, {\n  nodeKey: 'id',\n  source: 'source',\n  target: 'target',\n  forces: [\n    { type: 'manyBody', strength: -80 },\n    {\n      type: 'custom',\n      name: 'radial',\n      create: () => forceRadial(120, 0, 0).strength(0.08),\n    },\n  ],\n})\n```\n\n`create` receives one `ForceFactoryContext` with the private mutable node and\nlink clones, immutable resolved endpoint-key arrays, and a `nodeKey` accessor\ncompatible with `d3.forceLink().id(...)`. The factory must return a D3 `Force`.\nThe working-clone types reserve D3's simulation fields; valid numeric node\nseeds are retained, while conflicting source fields cannot leak into D3 state.\nNames are nonempty and unique across custom and built-in forces. Factories may\nconfigure or close over the private records, but must not add, remove, or\nreorder them. The transform validates collection identity and final finite\ncoordinates.\n\nThe transform clones its inputs before D3 mutates simulation state. Output\nnodes retain non-reserved source fields and add `x`, `y`, `vx`, `vy`, `source`,\nand `sourceIndexes`. Output links retain their raw endpoint keys and add\nresolved node references, source and target indexes, `x1`, `y1`, `x2`, `y2`,\n`sourceRows`, and `sourceIndexes`. The result also contains `xDomain` and\n`yDomain` for configured positional scales.\n\nNode keys must be unique, and every link endpoint must match one. Stable input\nand deterministic force factories produce repeatable static settlement. The\nsimulation remains stopped and ticks synchronously; a custom force does not\ngain a timer or live-state ownership. The transform is eager, chart-size\nindependent, and uncached; memoize it with other derived data when a framework\ncomponent rebuilds unchanged input.\n\nThis API does not run a live simulation or own drag state. Products that need\ncontinuous physics or node dragging should keep that controller and its\npositions in application state, then render the current rows through normal\nmarks.\n\n## Lineage\n\nAggregations expose `source` and `sourceIndexes`. Row-extending transforms\nexpose the source rows used for that derived value. This supports inspection,\ntooltips, drill-down, and subsequent transforms without renderer knowledge.\n\nLineage is direct to the immediate input. In a `fold` → `normalize` pipeline,\nthe normalized row points to its folded input, and that folded row points to\nthe original source row. `select` returns the chosen input rows unchanged.\n\nSee [Transforms and Reactivity](../guides/transforms-and-reactivity.md) for\ncomposition and memoization guidance.\n\n## Types\n\nValue and grouping contracts are `TransformAccessor`,\n`TransformAccessorContext`, `TransformField`, `TransformValue`,\n`TransformValueOutput`, `TransformKey`, `TransformGroupSpec`,\n`TransformGroupRow`, `TransformOrder`, `TransformOrderOptions`, and\n`TransformLineage`.\n\nReducer contracts are `TransformNumericReducer`, `TransformReducer`,\n`TransformReduceContext`, `TransformOutputSpec`, `TransformOutputs`,\n`TransformOutputValue`, and `TransformOutputRow`.\n\nGroup exports are `GroupByOptions` and `GroupByDatum`. Numeric bin exports are\n`BinOptions`, `BinXDatum`, and `BinYDatum`. Two-dimensional bin exports are\n`BinXYOptions` and `BinXYDatum`. Calendar bin exports are `TimeIntervalLike`,\n`BinTimeOptions`, and `BinTimeDatum`.\n\nFold exports are `FoldField`, `FoldOutputNames`, `FoldOptions`, and\n`FoldDatum`.\n\nRolling exports are `RollingWindowOptions`, `RollingWindowDatum`, and `RollingWindowAnchor`.\nCumulative exports are `CumulativeOptions` and `CumulativeDatum`. Rank exports\nare `RankOptions`, `RankDatum`, and `RankTies`.\n\nNormalization exports are `NormalizeOptions`, `NormalizeDatum`,\n`NormalizeBasis`, and `NormalizeContext`. Selection exports are\n`SelectOptions`, `SelectMethod`, and `SelectContext`. Row-stack exports are\n`StackRowsXOptions`, `StackRowsXDatum`, `StackRowsYOptions`, and\n`StackRowsYDatum`.\n\nMosaic exports are `MosaicOptions`, `MosaicXDatum`, and `MosaicYDatum`.\n\nWaterfall exports are `WaterfallKind`, `WaterfallOptions`, `WaterfallDatum`,\n`WaterfallStepDatum`, and `WaterfallTotalDatum`.\n\nStatic force-layout exports are `forceLayout`, `ForceLayoutOptions`,\n`ForceDescriptor`, `ForceNumericValue`, `ForceLinkDescriptor`,\n`ForceManyBodyDescriptor`, `ForceCenterDescriptor`, `ForceCollideDescriptor`,\n`ForceXDescriptor`, `ForceYDescriptor`, `ForceFactoryDescriptor`,\n`ForceFactory`, `ForceFactoryContext`, `ForceLayoutWorkingNode`,\n`ForceLayoutWorkingLink`, `ForceLayoutResult`, `ForceLayoutNode`,\n`ForceLayoutLink`, and `ForceLinkLineage`.\n\nTidy-tree exports are `treeLayout`, `TreeOrientation`, `TreeNodeContext`,\n`TreeNodeComparator`, `TreeNodeSeparation`, `TreeLayoutPathOptions`,\n`TreeLayoutParentOptions`, `TreeLayoutOptions`, `TreeLayoutNode`,\n`TreeLayoutLink`, and `TreeLayoutResult`.\n"}
